WEBVTT

00:01.311 --> 00:04.501
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.526 --> 00:07.840
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:07.932 --> 00:11.001
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.052 --> 00:13.971
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.022 --> 00:17.891
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:17.976 --> 00:20.844
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.398 --> 00:22.932
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.047 --> 00:24.347
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.415 --> 00:28.186
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:35.189 --> 00:36.120
Oh, hey,
there you are.

00:36.121 --> 00:38.155
Hey, listen, I got something
really cool I want you to see.

00:38.223 --> 00:39.256
What is it?

00:39.324 --> 00:41.191
This.
I found it in the yard.

00:41.259 --> 00:42.226
A tennis ball.

00:42.293 --> 00:44.428
That's very impressive, Brian.

00:44.496 --> 00:46.230
Yeah, but don't tell the other
dogs in the neighborhood.

00:46.297 --> 00:47.264
They'll be jealous.

00:47.332 --> 00:49.133
You son of a bitch.

00:49.200 --> 00:50.300
And this is nothing.

00:50.368 --> 00:51.568
There's one tennis
ball out there

00:51.636 --> 00:53.137
that's even cooler
'cause I peed on it.

00:53.204 --> 00:54.271
Buried it years ago.

00:54.339 --> 00:55.639
I just can't
remember where.

00:55.707 --> 00:57.141
I'd give anything
to find it, though.

00:57.208 --> 00:59.209
Well, if you could recall
exactly when you buried it,

00:59.277 --> 01:00.644
I suppose I could
help you out.

01:00.712 --> 01:02.112
I remember the exact day.

01:02.180 --> 01:04.415
January 31, 1999.

01:04.482 --> 01:06.417
Very well, follow me, Brian.

01:06.484 --> 01:08.252
Watch out for Ernie.

01:08.319 --> 01:11.121
He's getting a CAT scan.

01:26.771 --> 01:29.339
Well, here we are.

01:29.407 --> 01:30.908
That's odd.

01:30.975 --> 01:33.410
It's our house, but somehow
it looks a little different.

01:33.478 --> 01:35.212
MEG: Mom, my lips
are too thin.

01:35.280 --> 01:37.014
Can I please get
collagen injections?

01:37.082 --> 01:39.216
Meg, you don't need
to change the way you look.

01:39.284 --> 01:40.818
You know, most of
the world's problems

01:40.885 --> 01:42.720
stem from poor self-image.

01:42.787 --> 01:44.221
Oh, my God,
what's with Meg's voice?

01:44.289 --> 01:45.656
She sounds like someone

01:45.724 --> 01:47.591
who's about to give up
a huge opportunity.

01:47.659 --> 01:49.093
That's nothing.
Look at you.

01:49.160 --> 01:51.829
You look like a prize at some
Mexican church carnival.

01:51.896 --> 01:53.063
Excellent.

01:53.131 --> 01:54.798
The mind control device
is nearing completion.

01:54.866 --> 01:57.401
Stewie, I said
no toys at the table.

01:57.469 --> 01:58.669
Damn you, vile woman!

01:58.737 --> 02:00.304
You've impeded my work
since the day

02:00.371 --> 02:02.039
I escaped from
your wretched womb.

02:02.107 --> 02:03.574
Oh, don't pout, honey.

02:03.641 --> 02:06.376
You know, when you were born,
the doctor said you were

02:06.444 --> 02:08.812
the happiest looking baby
he'd ever seen.

02:08.880 --> 02:09.847
But of course.

02:09.914 --> 02:11.315
That was my victory day.

02:11.382 --> 02:13.283
The fruition
of my deeply-laid plans

02:13.351 --> 02:15.986
to escape from that cursed
ovarian bastille.

02:16.054 --> 02:17.521
Return the device, woman!

02:17.589 --> 02:19.690
Geez. What, did you carry
a thesaurus around with you?

02:19.758 --> 02:21.525
You know, it's amazing
I could speak at all

02:21.593 --> 02:23.060
with that circumcision
still healing.

02:23.128 --> 02:24.194
Hey, wait a minute, Stewie.

02:24.262 --> 02:25.362
That's the spot!

02:25.430 --> 02:26.697
That's where I buried
my tennis ball!

02:26.765 --> 02:27.798
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

02:27.866 --> 02:28.799
Brian, what
are you doing?

02:28.867 --> 02:30.200
You can't dig
it up here.

02:30.268 --> 02:32.069
Just make a mental note
of where you buried it,

02:32.137 --> 02:33.303
and as soon as we're
back in the present,

02:33.371 --> 02:34.404
you can dig it up then.

02:34.472 --> 02:35.939
- Why?
- Because we can't risk

02:36.007 --> 02:37.708
doing anything that
alters the past.

02:37.776 --> 02:39.042
You know, I never got that.

02:39.110 --> 02:41.478
I mean, w-wouldn't you want
to alter the past?

02:41.546 --> 02:43.714
I mean, you could make life
better for yourself.

02:43.782 --> 02:45.716
And not just for yourself,
for everyone.

02:45.784 --> 02:47.751
You-You could stop 9/11.

02:47.819 --> 02:50.254
Brian, there's one rule
of time travel, and that is:

02:50.321 --> 02:52.222
Do not alter the past in any way

02:52.290 --> 02:53.957
or the consequences
could be dire.

02:54.025 --> 02:54.958
Yeah, where'd
you hear that?

02:55.026 --> 02:56.160
<i>Quantum Leap.</i>

02:56.227 --> 02:58.061
That guy changed the past
all the time!

02:58.129 --> 02:59.329
BRIAN:
Whoa, ass ahoy.

03:00.632 --> 03:01.765
Hey, Peter, it's 7:00

03:01.833 --> 03:03.066
and you've still
got your pants on.

03:03.134 --> 03:04.034
What's the occasion?

03:04.102 --> 03:05.402
Ew, I remember this.

03:05.470 --> 03:07.337
Peter's eye did
that weird creepy thing

03:07.405 --> 03:08.906
where it went
over his nose.

03:08.973 --> 03:10.874
Come on, you're
worrying about nothing.

03:10.942 --> 03:12.743
Oh? Remember when
you got drunk

03:12.811 --> 03:14.478
off the communion wine
at church?

03:14.546 --> 03:15.579
STEWIE:
Ah! Ew! Gross!

03:15.647 --> 03:16.713
Look at that.

03:16.781 --> 03:19.116
Wait, what are
they doing?

03:19.184 --> 03:20.284
I don't know.

03:20.351 --> 03:22.252
They're just standing there
like zombies.

03:22.320 --> 03:23.687
Do you think
they're all right?

03:23.755 --> 03:26.123
I-I'm not sure.

03:26.191 --> 03:28.725
And then there was that time
at the ice cream store.

03:30.962 --> 03:32.329
They're doing it again.
What the hell?

03:32.397 --> 03:33.697
I-I'm at a loss.

03:33.765 --> 03:35.532
I mean, I know we're not
supposed to mess with

03:35.600 --> 03:37.901
the timeline, but should
we call an ambulance?

03:37.969 --> 03:41.805
That is so creepy.

03:41.873 --> 03:43.740
And remember you had
an Irish coffee

03:43.808 --> 03:46.310
<i>the day we went
to see Philadelphia?</i>

03:46.377 --> 03:48.645
Oh. They're do-- they're
setting up (bleep) cutaways.

03:48.713 --> 03:50.647
Oh, my God, is that
what we did back then?

03:50.715 --> 03:52.082
Yeah, I mean,
now, we just like

03:52.150 --> 03:54.551
return text messages
and screw around and whatnot.

03:54.619 --> 03:56.687
Lois, I'm not going
back to work tomorrow.

03:56.754 --> 03:58.388
That new boss
has it in for me.

03:58.456 --> 04:00.757
He's meaner than
a shifty salesman.

04:07.999 --> 04:09.499
You sure you got time
to smoke?

04:09.567 --> 04:10.934
Oh, yeah,
it's an Al Harrington.

04:11.002 --> 04:12.569
It goes on for a while.

04:12.637 --> 04:14.771
All right, look, we found out
where your ball is buried.

04:14.839 --> 04:16.273
Now let's get back
to our own time.

04:16.341 --> 04:17.708
Okay, get the return pad.
Let's go.

04:17.775 --> 04:18.775
Not out here
in the open.

04:18.843 --> 04:20.043
Someone could see us.

04:20.111 --> 04:21.812
Remember? No altering
the timeline?

04:21.880 --> 04:24.014
Come on, let's
get up to my room.

04:24.082 --> 04:26.216
Hey, wait a minute, Stewie,
I got to take a leak.

04:26.284 --> 04:27.584
Will it screw up
the timeline

04:27.652 --> 04:29.553
if my future pee goes
in a past toilet?

04:29.621 --> 04:31.755
Uh, no, as long as
it goes in the toilet.

04:31.823 --> 04:33.490
One splash on
a magazine in there,

04:33.558 --> 04:35.893
and we're getting
chased by dinosaurs.

04:37.829 --> 04:39.763
Huh. Looks more or less
the same.

04:39.831 --> 04:41.865
Oh, my God, my Japanese
children's books.

04:41.933 --> 04:43.400
I forgot about these.

04:43.468 --> 04:44.968
<i>You Poop Now.</i>

04:45.036 --> 04:47.204
<i>Horton Hears a Suicide.</i>

04:47.272 --> 04:50.007
<i>The Little Engine That Will,
Or Get Great Shame.</i>

04:50.074 --> 04:51.074
Oh, there you are.

04:51.142 --> 04:52.109
All right,
come on, Brian.

04:52.176 --> 04:53.210
Let's get
out of here.

04:53.278 --> 04:54.478
STEWIE:
Blast, that draggle-tailed,

04:54.545 --> 04:55.979
blunt-edged,
matriarchal despot!

04:56.047 --> 04:57.948
Who the deuce
does she think she is?!

04:58.016 --> 04:58.949
Quick, Brian, hide!

05:00.852 --> 05:03.987
I shall cleverly disguise this
lethal plasma disruptor device

05:04.055 --> 05:06.323
as a tuna fish sandwich,
whereupon I shall end

05:06.391 --> 05:08.692
that wretched woman's
Draconian reign of tyranny!

05:11.129 --> 05:12.095
(sneezes)

05:12.163 --> 05:13.697
(gasps)

05:16.901 --> 05:18.602
Oh. Hey.

05:18.670 --> 05:19.903
Who the deuce
are you?

05:19.971 --> 05:23.040
Uh, well, I'm, uh...
I'm you.

05:23.107 --> 05:25.642
You look like me, but
that's utterly impossible.

05:25.710 --> 05:27.010
I'm afraid it's true.

05:27.078 --> 05:28.345
I'm you from the future.

05:28.413 --> 05:29.613
I've traveled back in time.

05:29.681 --> 05:30.981
That's absurd.

05:31.049 --> 05:33.383
Look, I can prove to you
that I'm you from the future.

05:33.451 --> 05:35.986
I know about
the Eddie Bauer catalog.

05:36.054 --> 05:37.587
(gasps)
It is you!

05:37.655 --> 05:39.589
Okay, bye, Lois.

05:39.657 --> 05:42.125
I'm going to the stag party.

05:42.193 --> 05:43.627
Brian, you might as well
come out now.

05:43.695 --> 05:44.661
He knows everything.

05:45.496 --> 05:46.596
(screams)

05:46.664 --> 05:48.265
(grunts)

05:48.333 --> 05:49.299
Uh-oh.

06:07.618 --> 06:08.885
Brian, are you
all right?

06:08.953 --> 06:09.886
Yeah, I'm fine.

06:09.954 --> 06:10.988
Where'd you get that?

06:11.055 --> 06:12.255
The Stewie from
the past has

06:12.323 --> 06:13.957
a lot more gadgets
and things than I do.

06:14.025 --> 06:15.359
I've kind of slacked
off a little bit.

06:15.426 --> 06:17.361
How's the party?
Looks like fun.

06:17.428 --> 06:19.196
Hey, who wants to play
"Drink the Beer"?

06:19.263 --> 06:20.230
Right here.

06:20.298 --> 06:21.665
- You win!
- All right!

06:21.733 --> 06:23.633
- What do I win?
- Another beer!

06:23.701 --> 06:26.570
Okay, take a last look,
'cause we're out of here.

06:26.637 --> 06:28.038
Oh, look at that.

06:28.106 --> 06:29.906
There's the old gang
we've gotten to know

06:29.974 --> 06:31.375
so well over the years.

06:34.278 --> 06:35.879
TV's not even
plugged in.

06:40.752 --> 06:41.685
(crowd cheering)

06:41.753 --> 06:42.786
What is this?

06:42.854 --> 06:44.121
Where the
hell are we?

06:44.188 --> 06:45.555
JOHN MADDEN (over P.A.):
The air is electric

06:45.623 --> 06:48.025
here at Super Bowl 33 tonight.

06:48.092 --> 06:49.659
- Stewie, what's going on?
- I don't know.

06:49.727 --> 06:50.994
For some reason,
the time machine

06:51.062 --> 06:52.229
didn't take us
back to the present.

06:52.296 --> 06:53.730
We're still in 1999.

06:53.798 --> 06:55.399
Wait a minute.
I remember this.

06:55.466 --> 06:57.167
I think I'm up
in that blimp.

06:59.437 --> 07:01.104
Amazing. You can
barely drive a car,

07:01.172 --> 07:02.973
and yet you were
allowed to fly a blimp?

07:03.041 --> 07:04.007
Yeah, America's
great, isn't it?

07:04.075 --> 07:05.409
'Cept for the South.

07:05.476 --> 07:08.078
Oh, no wonder the return pad
didn't take us all the way.

07:08.146 --> 07:09.780
The batteries
are almost depleted.

07:09.847 --> 07:11.048
Are those D batteries?

07:11.115 --> 07:12.716
Yes, ever since we had
that trouble in Germany,

07:12.784 --> 07:14.151
I found a way to convert it

07:14.218 --> 07:16.253
so it takes D batteries
instead of uranium.

07:16.320 --> 07:18.388
Only problem is, I didn't
bring any cash with me.

07:18.456 --> 07:19.723
Did you?

07:19.791 --> 07:21.091
I don't think that's
gonna be a problem.

07:22.794 --> 07:24.327
Oh, my God!
Quick, grab some money!

07:26.531 --> 07:28.265
Do you, do you
have pockets?

07:28.332 --> 07:29.766
Are you putting that
in pockets?

07:29.834 --> 07:31.001
That's so cute.

07:32.403 --> 07:35.238
(clamoring)

07:35.306 --> 07:37.040
JOHN MADDEN (over P.A.):
The crowd is storming the field!

07:37.108 --> 07:38.408
This is pandemonium!

07:39.944 --> 07:43.046
(gunfire)

07:43.114 --> 07:44.714
(air hissing)

07:47.919 --> 07:49.553
(both scream)

07:51.456 --> 07:53.323
Quick, Brian, get on!

07:59.163 --> 08:00.697
BRIAN:
Whoa, crash ahoy.

08:04.602 --> 08:06.903
Oh, God!
Now where are we?

08:09.207 --> 08:12.509
Well, you know, I figured
the sooner I cashed the check,

08:12.577 --> 08:14.344
the sooner they'd, uh,
catch their mistake.

08:14.412 --> 08:15.879
(chuckles)

08:15.947 --> 08:17.848
Look, why are we making
a federal case out of this?

08:17.915 --> 08:19.249
Oh, man, this
is the day

08:19.317 --> 08:21.118
Peter went to court
for his welfare fraud.

08:21.185 --> 08:22.252
(coughing)

08:24.322 --> 08:25.255
Hey.

08:25.323 --> 08:26.857
Huh? Oh, hey.

08:26.924 --> 08:28.058
You're the Kool-Aid Guy.

08:28.126 --> 08:29.092
Yeah.

08:29.160 --> 08:30.260
What are you doing?

08:30.328 --> 08:31.328
Just waiting.

08:31.395 --> 08:32.529
For what?

08:32.597 --> 08:33.964
I'm just waiting,
dude-- relax.

08:34.031 --> 08:36.433
Hey, is there any place around
here that sells batteries?

08:36.501 --> 08:37.667
Guys, d-don't
distract me, all right?

08:37.735 --> 08:39.136
I gotta really be up.

08:39.203 --> 08:42.105
Mr. Griffin, I think your
words have touched us all.

08:42.173 --> 08:44.808
I'm sentencing you
to 24 months in prison!

08:44.876 --> 08:46.243
Oh, no.
Oh, no.

08:46.310 --> 08:47.477
Oh, no!
Oh, no!

08:50.148 --> 08:52.149
So there's no organs
or glands or anything, right?

08:52.216 --> 08:53.383
It's just the liquid?

08:53.451 --> 08:54.718
Yeah. Yeah, it's
just the liquid.

08:54.785 --> 08:56.953
Um, I don't know
if this is a weird question,

08:57.021 --> 08:59.089
but... can I have some?

08:59.157 --> 09:00.690
All right, fine.
Tilt your head back,

09:00.758 --> 09:02.592
and I'll lean my liquid
down into your mouth.

09:02.660 --> 09:04.060
Oh, now I don't want it.

09:04.128 --> 09:05.462
Oh, crap!
Did I miss it?!

09:05.530 --> 09:08.798
Look, my husband may be
a bit thoughtless at times;

09:08.866 --> 09:11.835
he may even be downright,
well, stupid,

09:11.903 --> 09:15.405
but I know he only accepted
that money because he...

09:15.473 --> 09:17.841
Oh, yeah! Oh, yeah! Yeah!

09:28.252 --> 09:29.553
You guys did this!

09:29.620 --> 09:31.288
You guys (bleep) did this!

09:31.355 --> 09:32.689
Talking about my guts?!

09:32.757 --> 09:34.124
(bleep) me up?!
(bleep) you!

09:40.199 --> 09:41.104
Okay, this is good.

09:41.105 --> 09:42.238
We got the
D batteries.

09:42.306 --> 09:43.306
Let's get back
to the present.

09:43.374 --> 09:44.874
All right, get on.

09:51.415 --> 09:52.849
(sighs)
We made it.

09:52.916 --> 09:54.317
Yeah, the Super Bowl
was cool,

09:54.385 --> 09:57.220
but frankly, no Tommy Brady,
no Stewie likey.

09:57.287 --> 09:58.655
Brian, there you are!

09:58.722 --> 10:00.156
Come quick,
they're about to announce

10:00.224 --> 10:02.291
the unveiling of
your statue on the news.

10:02.359 --> 10:03.793
Statue?! For what?

10:03.861 --> 10:05.928
What do you think,
Mr. National Hero?

10:05.996 --> 10:09.599
For stopping the 9/11 terrorist
attacks and saving our country.

10:09.667 --> 10:11.034
Oh, you--

10:11.101 --> 10:14.404
I can't even express
how mad I am at you right now.

10:14.471 --> 10:15.772
If it wasn't for you,

10:15.839 --> 10:18.207
those planes would've hit
the World Trade Center.

10:18.275 --> 10:19.842
Brian, what the hell
did you do?!

10:19.910 --> 10:23.713
I, uh, may have told my
former self about 9/11.

10:23.781 --> 10:25.715
What did I tell you
about altering the past?!

10:25.783 --> 10:27.350
Wait-Wait, when did you
even do that?

10:27.418 --> 10:29.519
Well, you remember when I said
I was going to take a leak?

10:29.586 --> 10:31.054
Brian, you shouldn't
have done that.

10:31.121 --> 10:33.690
Who knows what unforeseen
consequences are awaiting us.

10:33.757 --> 10:35.491
Saddam Hussein
could be president.

10:35.559 --> 10:37.860
Mexico could be the world's
dominant super power.

10:37.928 --> 10:39.962
Cookie Monster could have
invented Facebook!

10:40.030 --> 10:41.497
What is this?

10:41.565 --> 10:42.932
Cookiebook.

10:43.000 --> 10:43.900
LOIS:
Brian!

10:43.967 --> 10:45.301
Hurry, it's starting!

10:47.438 --> 10:48.404
Good evening.
I'm Tom Tucker.

10:48.472 --> 10:49.972
And I'm Joyce Kinney.

10:50.040 --> 10:52.709
Our top story: Quahog prepares
to unveil a new statue

10:52.776 --> 10:54.677
commemorating perhaps
its greatest hero ever.

10:54.745 --> 10:56.379
That's right, Tom.

10:56.447 --> 10:59.148
It was ten years ago that an
almost inconceivable plot

10:59.216 --> 11:00.750
to destroy the
World Trade Center

11:00.818 --> 11:03.419
was thwarted by Quahog's
own Brian Griffin.

11:03.487 --> 11:05.955
This amateur video
captured Griffin's heroism

11:06.023 --> 11:08.458
in the face of a shadowy
terrorist organization called--

11:08.525 --> 11:10.359
holy (bleep),
look at all those vowels--

11:10.427 --> 11:13.763
Al Qua-aye-eeda.

11:13.831 --> 11:15.998
Here.
Make sure you get all this.

11:16.066 --> 11:17.834
(indistinct outing)

11:17.901 --> 11:20.837
Time to terrorize
the terrorists.

11:20.904 --> 11:23.306
Ugh. You prepared
catch phrases for yourself?

11:23.373 --> 11:25.208
No. Not necessarily.

11:27.411 --> 11:29.712
Mohammed Atta stayed home.

11:29.780 --> 11:31.114
Nobody knows
that guy's name yet.

11:31.181 --> 11:33.416
You're using information
that nobody knows.

11:34.852 --> 11:37.253
Houston, we have a solution.

11:37.321 --> 11:39.856
Houston's for space,
not everyday air travel.

11:39.923 --> 11:42.391
Tell them to ground every plane
out of the East Coast.

11:42.459 --> 11:43.793
Seacrest out.

11:43.861 --> 11:45.695
(passengers cheering)

11:48.132 --> 11:49.665
Wow. What a hero.

11:49.733 --> 11:51.434
Coming up next in sports:

11:51.502 --> 11:53.236
Arizona Cardinal
Pat Tillman

11:53.303 --> 11:55.304
tackled by his own team?

11:55.372 --> 11:56.973
Oh, Brian,
we're so proud of you.

11:57.040 --> 11:58.174
Yeah, buddy.

11:58.242 --> 11:59.609
Great job stopping
those terrorists.

11:59.676 --> 12:01.110
And thank God
their follow-up attack

12:01.178 --> 12:02.912
on St. Louis was a bust.

12:04.815 --> 12:06.115
<i>MAN:
We missed!</i>

12:06.183 --> 12:08.851
Brian, I promise you,
all of this can only end badly.

12:08.919 --> 12:10.153
Stewie, it's fine.

12:10.220 --> 12:11.621
I prevented 9/11.

12:11.688 --> 12:13.489
How could that possibly
be a bad thing?

12:13.557 --> 12:15.691
I mean, come on, I-I
saved, like, 200 lives.

12:15.759 --> 12:16.759
3,000.

12:16.827 --> 12:18.294
Wow! 3,000?!

12:18.362 --> 12:20.396
Oh, you knew what you
were doing there.

12:20.464 --> 12:22.431
TOM: This major
breaking news just in.

12:22.499 --> 12:24.000
Nine Southern states
have declared

12:24.067 --> 12:25.835
that they are seceding
from the United States.

12:25.903 --> 12:27.036
The announcement came

12:27.104 --> 12:29.005
from former President
George W. Bush,

12:29.072 --> 12:30.540
who reformed
the Confederacy

12:30.607 --> 12:33.876
after a bitter loss in
his 2004 reelection bid.

12:33.944 --> 12:35.578
Bush lost in 2004?

12:35.646 --> 12:36.712
Yeah, he probably
couldn't

12:36.780 --> 12:38.815
exploit people's
fears with no 9/11.

12:38.882 --> 12:40.049
Bush appeared
before the press today

12:40.117 --> 12:41.651
to make this brief statement.

12:41.718 --> 12:42.985
If anyone out there can get

12:43.053 --> 12:44.754
their hands
on a clown's suitcase,

12:44.822 --> 12:47.056
I'd very much like to see
what's inside of there.

12:47.124 --> 12:49.358
I think we have the wrong clip.

12:49.426 --> 12:51.694
What? No?

12:51.762 --> 12:53.529
It... this...?

12:53.597 --> 12:55.698
Uh, okay,
that was George W. Bush

12:55.766 --> 12:57.834
declaring civil war
on the Northern states.

12:57.901 --> 12:59.936
Oh, my God!
Civil war?

13:00.003 --> 13:01.337
See, Brian?

13:01.405 --> 13:02.772
This is exactly
what I was talking about.

13:02.840 --> 13:04.340
Look, Stewie, this
doesn't necessarily mean

13:04.408 --> 13:05.875
things are going
to turn out badly.

13:05.943 --> 13:07.577
In fact, I bet, five
years from now,

13:07.644 --> 13:09.812
the world will still be
better off for what I did.

13:09.880 --> 13:11.180
Is that so?

13:11.248 --> 13:12.849
Well, Brian, if you're
that sure of yourself,

13:12.916 --> 13:14.851
then let's go five years
into the future,

13:14.918 --> 13:16.686
and you can prove me wrong.
Fine.

13:28.332 --> 13:29.999
Wow. This is
the future?

13:30.067 --> 13:31.934
Everything
looks slicker.

13:32.002 --> 13:33.769
Hey, Lois, I found some
double-sided tape.

13:33.837 --> 13:35.738
I think I can do about
seven minutes worth

13:35.806 --> 13:37.206
of funny stuff
with it.

13:37.274 --> 13:38.975
That should get us to the
Meg kissing booth story.

13:39.042 --> 13:41.544
Wow. Things have gotten
kind of lazy, huh?

13:41.612 --> 13:42.845
Hey, Peter.

13:42.913 --> 13:45.014
Thanks for letting me
borrow your screwdriver set.

13:45.082 --> 13:47.483
You know, I just want to say
how nice it is

13:47.551 --> 13:49.819
to be back here with y'all
on Spooner Street.

13:49.887 --> 13:52.388
Oh, I guess things
didn't work out in Virginia.

13:52.456 --> 13:54.624
Chris, I heard you got a
"D" on your report card.

13:54.691 --> 13:55.958
Here's a cutaway.

13:56.026 --> 13:58.761
Matthew McConaughey is terrible.

13:58.829 --> 14:00.129
Good Lord!
What's happened to us?

14:00.197 --> 14:01.764
Okay, maybe things
seem a little off,

14:01.832 --> 14:03.666
but other than that,
the world seems okay.

14:03.734 --> 14:05.101
Which proves my point.

14:05.168 --> 14:07.703
Me stopping 9/11 led to a
world that's just fine.

14:07.771 --> 14:09.805
Well, I'll see y'all tomorrow.

14:11.541 --> 14:14.076
(heavy gunfire,
explosions booming)

14:17.481 --> 14:20.016
Brian, you might want
to look outside.

14:20.083 --> 14:21.884
Oh, my God!

14:27.324 --> 14:30.226
(Cleveland screaming)

14:30.294 --> 14:32.161
Oh, oh! Pebble in my shoe!

14:32.229 --> 14:34.630
Pebble in my shoe.
Pebble in my shoe.

14:34.698 --> 14:36.098
Nice work, Bri.

14:36.166 --> 14:37.867
Whoops.

14:37.935 --> 14:40.069
Well, at least Joe
gets to be a cyborg.

14:42.439 --> 14:44.640
(mechanical whirring)

14:47.477 --> 14:48.778
Freeze, Frogmire.

14:48.845 --> 14:50.780
You are out past curfew,

14:50.847 --> 14:53.516
and therefore, in violation
of local ordinance.

14:53.583 --> 14:54.417
Ribbity.

15:02.791 --> 15:04.258
What the hell happened?

15:04.326 --> 15:06.360
Could I really have
caused all this?

15:06.428 --> 15:08.529
It says here that the new
American civil war

15:08.597 --> 15:10.631
you set into motion
ended up in a series

15:10.699 --> 15:13.634
of concentrated nuclear strikes
all along the Eastern Seaboard.

15:13.702 --> 15:16.036
It killed 17 million people.

15:16.104 --> 15:17.371
Oh.

15:17.439 --> 15:19.006
- Including Cesar Millan.
- No!

15:19.074 --> 15:20.874
Okay, okay, I get it--
Stewie, I screwed up.

15:20.942 --> 15:22.509
All right? I shouldn't
have altered the past.

15:22.577 --> 15:23.844
Is there anything
we can do?

15:23.912 --> 15:25.913
Well, the only way
to fix this is to return

15:25.981 --> 15:28.148
to the moment when we
first arrived in the past

15:28.216 --> 15:30.484
and stop you from spilling
the beans about 9/11.

15:30.552 --> 15:32.186
Okay. Okay,
let's do it.

15:38.159 --> 15:39.393
BRIAN (inside house):
Whoa, ass ahoy.

15:39.461 --> 15:40.794
All right, look,
there we are.

15:40.862 --> 15:42.262
BRIAN: Hey, uh,
Peter, it's 7:00

15:42.330 --> 15:43.697
and you've still
got your pants on.

15:43.765 --> 15:44.632
What's the
occasion?

15:44.699 --> 15:45.699
Stop!
Both of you!

15:45.767 --> 15:46.900
What the hell?
Who are you?

15:46.968 --> 15:48.268
We're you,
from the future.

15:48.336 --> 15:50.037
Oh, boy, this can't
be good news.

15:50.105 --> 15:51.939
Who screwed up?
Take a wild guess.

15:52.007 --> 15:54.008
- What did I do?
- Well, nothing yet, but listen.

15:54.075 --> 15:55.576
Whatever you do,
you cannot tell

15:55.644 --> 15:58.145
your former self--
that Brian-- about 9/11.

15:58.213 --> 16:01.215
If you do, America as you
know it will cease to exist.

16:01.282 --> 16:02.449
My God, Brian.
What the hell?

16:02.517 --> 16:03.651
We've been here
two minutes,

16:03.718 --> 16:05.352
and already you've
destroyed America.

16:05.420 --> 16:07.321
Okay, okay. I promise
I won't say anything.

16:07.389 --> 16:10.357
Also, if you don't give
that Stewie a back rub,

16:10.425 --> 16:12.159
it really messes
everything up.

16:14.663 --> 16:16.463
All right,
can we go now?

16:21.202 --> 16:22.770
Did that do it?

16:22.837 --> 16:23.837
Did we restore
the past?

16:23.905 --> 16:24.872
Well, let's check.

16:24.939 --> 16:27.441
"9/11."

16:27.509 --> 16:28.776
Oh, there it is.

16:28.843 --> 16:30.110
We did it, Brian.

16:30.178 --> 16:31.545
We made 9/11 happen.

16:31.613 --> 16:32.880
High five! All right!
All right!

16:32.947 --> 16:34.214
Yeah!
High five!

16:34.282 --> 16:35.349
Wow, that...
that probably

16:35.417 --> 16:37.084
wouldn't look very good
out of context.

16:37.152 --> 16:39.687
<i>There's the writer
of the Harry Potter series.</i>

16:39.754 --> 16:42.523
Must be weird hanging out
with us Muggles, huh, Brian?

16:42.590 --> 16:43.524
Yeah.

16:43.591 --> 16:45.292
Well, laundrium insertum.

16:45.360 --> 16:46.660
Huh? (chuckles)

16:46.728 --> 16:48.095
Oh, my God.

16:48.163 --> 16:49.596
All right, you obviously
have no self-control,

16:49.664 --> 16:51.999
so we've got to go back
and do this again.

16:54.636 --> 16:55.469
BRIAN:
Whoa, ass ahoy.

16:55.537 --> 16:56.570
All right, let's go.

16:56.638 --> 16:57.838
Hey, uh, Peter,
it's 7:00

16:57.906 --> 16:59.206
and you've still
got your pants on.

16:59.274 --> 17:00.841
Oh, yeah, that's right.
We were just here.

17:00.909 --> 17:02.309
BRIAN and STEWIE:
Wait!

17:02.377 --> 17:03.844
Who the devil
are you?

17:03.912 --> 17:05.412
We're from the future.
So are we.

17:05.480 --> 17:06.647
We're from
the further future.

17:06.715 --> 17:07.915
We came to stop you.

17:07.982 --> 17:09.216
Why?
What they did

17:09.284 --> 17:11.051
actually turns out
to be damage-free.

17:11.119 --> 17:12.853
It's what you're about to do--
stopping them--

17:12.921 --> 17:14.688
that causes utter bedlam.

17:14.756 --> 17:16.323
Oh, come on.

17:16.391 --> 17:17.558
Wait!
Stop!

17:17.625 --> 17:19.293
We're from the further
further future.

17:19.360 --> 17:21.295
What you guys do
eventually works out.

17:21.362 --> 17:22.863
It's what you two
are about to do

17:22.931 --> 17:24.264
that ruins everything!

17:24.332 --> 17:25.466
Stop!
Don't do anything!

17:25.533 --> 17:26.867
Oh, for
God's sake.

17:26.935 --> 17:28.802
We're from the further
further further future.

17:28.870 --> 17:30.370
What these two do
is fine.

17:30.438 --> 17:32.795
It's what you two do
that makes things worse.

17:32.796 --> 17:33.863
- Stop!
- Wait!

17:33.888 --> 17:34.508
- Hold it!
- Stop!

17:34.509 --> 17:35.809
- Wait!
- No!

17:35.877 --> 17:36.944
Hold it!
Stop!

17:37.011 --> 17:38.112
I don't know what
I'm doing here.

17:38.179 --> 17:39.079
I'm just looking
for the can.

17:39.147 --> 17:40.447
- Don't move!
- Hold it!

17:40.515 --> 17:41.448
- Freeze!
- Stop!

17:41.516 --> 17:42.516
- Wait!
- No!

17:42.584 --> 17:43.917
Oh, for God's sake, why?

17:43.985 --> 17:45.285
This is why!

17:45.353 --> 17:46.653
- Don't move!
- Hold it!

17:46.721 --> 17:47.821
- Freeze!
- Stop!

17:47.889 --> 17:49.223
- No!
- Wait!

17:49.290 --> 17:51.325
Oh, my God.
What's that?

17:51.392 --> 17:52.726
I don't know
what happened.

17:52.794 --> 17:54.595
This is life
for some reason.

17:54.662 --> 17:56.430
Don't do whatever
you're about to do.

17:56.498 --> 17:57.798
God help us.

17:57.866 --> 17:59.666
This is no
way to live.

17:59.734 --> 18:02.169
(both retching)

18:02.237 --> 18:04.304
(all talking and shouting
at once)

18:04.372 --> 18:06.073
No, don't do it!
Halt!

18:07.242 --> 18:10.444
(confused clamoring continues)

18:15.717 --> 18:19.186
All right, everybody,
shut the hell up!

18:19.254 --> 18:21.188
All right, this is (bleep),
all right?

18:21.256 --> 18:22.756
I can't follow
what's going on anymore,

18:22.824 --> 18:24.892
and I'm assuming
the rest of you can't, either.

18:24.959 --> 18:26.860
So let's make this easy
and take a vote.

18:26.928 --> 18:28.796
How many think
we should prevent 9/11?

18:28.863 --> 18:31.265
Raise your hands.

18:31.332 --> 18:33.133
One, two, three, four, five
six, seven, eight, nine...

18:33.201 --> 18:34.835
Okay, all right, looks like 42.

18:34.903 --> 18:36.503
All right, who votes "yes 9/11?"

18:38.640 --> 18:40.641
(muttering, counting rapidly)

18:40.708 --> 18:41.875
Okay, all right, 57.

18:41.943 --> 18:43.110
All right, 9/11 wins.

18:43.178 --> 18:44.745
Wait, wait, shouldn't it
be an even number?

18:44.813 --> 18:46.213
Why is the total
an odd number?

18:46.281 --> 18:48.348
Oh, yeah, I think one
of the Brians died.

18:48.416 --> 18:50.284
What? What do you mean?
I don't know-- one of them

18:50.351 --> 18:51.585
landed here
with its throat slit.

18:51.653 --> 18:53.220
But wait,
if one of me is dead,

18:53.288 --> 18:54.688
then shouldn't all
the me's be dead?

18:54.756 --> 18:56.690
Brian, I-I don't...
I... it's...

18:56.758 --> 18:58.158
I don't... I don't
know anymore. I-I...

18:58.226 --> 18:59.960
You know what?
I guess not. A-All right?

19:00.028 --> 19:01.929
Okay, listen up--
everybody, get back

19:01.996 --> 19:04.164
in your individual time machines
very carefully.

19:04.232 --> 19:05.566
Nobody touch anything.

19:05.633 --> 19:06.900
<i>No Harry Potter novels.</i>

19:06.968 --> 19:08.402
Don't step on a bug.

19:08.469 --> 19:10.404
Don't give earlier yous
investment advice.

19:10.471 --> 19:12.840
Just go back to where you
came from, and stay there!

19:32.093 --> 19:33.260
There.

19:33.328 --> 19:34.628
Now, we're going
to do what we

19:34.696 --> 19:36.029
should have done
in the first place.

19:36.097 --> 19:37.531
Get on.

19:40.802 --> 19:43.136
Wait a minute.
Isn't this where we just were?

19:43.204 --> 19:44.271
Where are
the other two us's?

19:44.339 --> 19:45.772
They're not here yet.

19:45.840 --> 19:48.008
Just to make sure there are
absolutely no loose ends,

19:48.076 --> 19:49.910
I've sent us back in time
to the moment

19:49.978 --> 19:52.012
just before you and I
originally arrived.

19:52.080 --> 19:53.747
Why?
You'll see.

19:53.815 --> 19:55.716
Get the (bleep) back
in your time machine!

19:55.783 --> 19:57.084
Aah!
Who are you?

19:57.151 --> 19:58.385
Never mind who I am!

19:58.453 --> 19:59.887
Just get back
in your time machine,

19:59.954 --> 20:01.822
get back to the present,
and stay there!

20:01.890 --> 20:03.724
Look, just calm down.
Aah!

20:03.791 --> 20:05.626
You gonna flap your lips
or you gonna do what I said?

20:05.693 --> 20:06.760
Okay, okay!

20:06.828 --> 20:08.896
All right! God!

20:08.963 --> 20:10.297
You just talked
to yourself.

20:10.365 --> 20:11.498
Won't that
alter the past?

20:11.566 --> 20:12.933
Don't worry, Brian.

20:13.001 --> 20:15.002
If I was successful
in restoring the past just now,

20:15.069 --> 20:16.770
you and I will never
have existed.

20:16.838 --> 20:18.572
The chronological tangent
that created us

20:18.640 --> 20:19.740
will have been erased.

20:19.807 --> 20:21.675
Oh. Is it going to hurt?

20:21.743 --> 20:23.644
Just a little.

20:23.711 --> 20:25.279
Ow.

20:32.086 --> 20:33.687
Oh, God, my leg
is killing me.

20:33.755 --> 20:35.522
Well, time travel
is risky.

20:35.613 --> 20:37.347
Every time you step
into that machine,

20:37.415 --> 20:38.515
you invite the
possibility

20:38.583 --> 20:40.350
of altering the
world as we know it.

20:40.418 --> 20:41.718
Thankfully, the
alternate versions of us

20:41.786 --> 20:43.253
were there to prevent
us from doing

20:43.321 --> 20:44.555
whatever it is we
apparently did.

20:45.036 --> 20:46.904
I wonder what it was.
We'll never know.

20:47.469 --> 20:48.836
But look on the
bright side.

20:48.904 --> 20:51.072
You might be sitting here
with a hole in your leg,

20:51.140 --> 20:52.907
but at least our present
universe hasn't been affected.

20:52.975 --> 20:54.576
PETER: Sorry, guys.
I'm gonna need the couch.

20:54.643 --> 20:56.777
The gang and I
are gonna watch the game.

20:57.150 --> 20:59.770
All right, let's unplug
the TV and get going.

20:59.771 --> 22:00.871
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru